Day 3 – Create a Subnet in the Default VPC (AWS)

Objective

Create a subnet named xfusion-subnet inside the default VPC.

Configuration
Setting                 Value
Subnet Name             xfusion-subnet
Subnet ID               subnet-0bcd1d7b17e53243e
VPC                     Default VPC (vpc-0e4a47901800250e5)
IPv4 CIDR               172.31.128.0/20
State                   Available
Block Public Access     Off

Steps Performed
1. Opened the AWS VPC Console.
2. Navigated to Virtual private cloud -> Subnets.
3. Clicked Create subnet.
4. Selected the default VPC.
5. Entered the subnet name (xfusion-subnet).
6. Entered the IPv4 CIDR block 172.31.128.0/20 (inside the VPC range 172.31.0.0/16).
7. Created the subnet; the console confirmed "You have successfully created 1 subnet".
8. Verified the subnet shows the Available state.
9. Reviewed the default Network ACL outbound rules.

Verification – Network ACL Outbound Rules (default)
Rule   Type          Protocol   Port Range   Destination   Action
100    All traffic   All        All          0.0.0.0/0     Allow
*      All traffic   All        All          0.0.0.0/0     Deny

Important Concepts Learned
- Subnet: A smaller IP range carved out of a VPC CIDR, tied to one Availability Zone. Resources such as EC2 instances are launched into subnets.
- Subnet CIDR (172.31.128.0/20): Provides 4,096 addresses (AWS reserves 5 per subnet), and must sit inside the VPC CIDR without overlapping other subnets.
- Network ACL (NACL): A stateless, subnet-level firewall. Rules are evaluated in number order; the final "*" rule denies anything not matched.
- NACL vs Security Group: NACLs work at the subnet level and are stateless; security groups work at the instance level and are stateful.
- Public vs private subnet: A subnet is public only if its route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to an Internet Gateway.

Key Takeaway
VPCs are divided into subnets, which organize and isolate resources. The default NACL allows all outbound traffic, so access is controlled mainly by security groups and route tables.
